.TH V32SIM 1 "November 2026" "v32sim 20261126-dev" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
v32sim \- headless simulator and single-step debugger for the Vircon32 fantasy console
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B v32sim
.RI [ OPTION ]...
.RI [ CARTFILE.v32 ]
.br
.B v32sim
.B \-\-version
.br
.B v32sim
.B \-\-help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B v32sim
runs a Vircon32 cartridge on its own models of the console's CPU, GPU, SPU,
timers, gamepads and memory card, and either stops at a prompt for
single-step debugging or runs unattended and reports what happened.
It reads the debug files written by the Vircon32 toolchain
.RB ( v32as " and " v32cc " with " \-g )
to show labels, source lines and variables.
.PP
.I CARTFILE.v32
is the cartridge; \fB\-\fR (or nothing) means no cartridge, and the BIOS runs
alone.
Without
.B \-r
the simulator stops before the first instruction and reads commands (see
.BR "DEBUGGER COMMANDS" ).
With
.BR \-r ,
.B \-\-batch
and
.BR \-x ,
it is a headless runner whose exit status says how the program ended.
.PP
Values given as
.IR OFFSET ,
.I OPCODE
and
.I ADDR
are hexadecimal.
Options with an optional value written
.RB [ =\fIX\fP ]
take it only in the attached form,
.BR \-\-name=\fIX\fP .
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Loading and BIOS
.TP
.BI \-B ", " \-\-biosfile= FILE
BIOS V32 file.
Default, in order:
.BR $V32BIOS ,
.IR /usr/local/Vircon32/Emulator/Bios/StandardBios.v32 ,
then the bundled
.I StubBios.v32
(see
.BR FILES ).
If none exists the StandardBios path is used, so the error message names it.
.B \-v
prints the one loaded.
.TP
.BI \-M ", " \-\-memcfile= FILE
Memory card file.
Changes are written back at the end of each frame.
.TP
.BI \-E ", " \-\-entry\-point= OFFSET
Start executing at
.I OFFSET
instead of the BIOS start.
.TP
.BR \-S ", " \-\-bios\-start
Start at the BIOS code start (the default).
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the usage text and exit.
.SS Debug information and source
.TP
.BI \-\-cart\-asm\-debug= FILE
.TP
.BI \-\-cart\-c\-debug= FILE
Load cartridge labels and line information from
.I FILE
(assembler
.I .vbin.debug
and compiler
.I .asm.debug
formats).
.TP
.BI \-\-bios\-asm\-debug= FILE
.TP
.BI \-\-bios\-c\-debug= FILE
The same for the BIOS.
.TP
.BI \-\-debug\-dir= DIR
Also look for debug files in
.IR DIR ,
which is tried first.
Otherwise they are searched for beside the V32 file, in its
.I obj/
and
.I ../obj/
and in
.I ./obj
and the current directory.
.TP
.BI \-\-symbol\-file= FILE
Also read data symbols from
.I FILE
(the
.I NAME.asm.sym
format of
.BR v32cc ).
The ones beside the cartridge are read automatically.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-no\-debug
Do not process any debug files.
.TP
.B \-\-source
Show the source line at each stop (assembly; C or Lua with
.BR \-L ).
.TP
.BI \-L ", " \-\-langmode= MODE
.I MODE
is
.BR C ,
.B lua
(Lua values in memory dumps) or
.B src
(any language).
.B C
and
.B src
also turn
.B \-\-source
on.
.B step
and
.B next
then go by source line.
Any other value is an error (message on standard error, exit status 1).
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-deref\-addr
Show the address of a dereference.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-colors
Colorized output at the prompt.
.SS Breakpoints, entry and watching
.TP
.BI \-b ", " \-\-break= OFFSET | LABEL | FILE:LINE
Set a breakpoint, as the
.B break
command does once the debug files are loaded; may be repeated.
A name that is a known label (or function) is that label even when it is also
a hexadecimal number (add, face, dead; write 0xdead for the address);
otherwise the value is a hexadecimal
.IR OFFSET ,
with or without 0x.
.I FILE:LINE
needs line information.
A value that is none of these is reported on standard error.
.TP
.BI \-w ", " \-\-watch\-for= OPCODE
Run (as
.BR \-r )
until this instruction word is executed.
.TP
.BI \-C ", " \-\-command\-file= FILE
Run the debugger commands in
.IR FILE .
They are read by the prompt after the first instruction is fetched, so
.BR step ,
.B next
and
.B continue
act; with
.B \-r
they run once at start.
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-errorcheck
Runtime error checking.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-profile
Instruction profiling; the
.B profile
command shows the report.
.SS Run control, speed and turbo
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-run
Do not start in single-step mode.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-frames= N
Stop after
.I N
frames, counted from power-on (StandardBios's logo, about 250 frames, included;
StubBios has none).
.TP
.B \-\-fast
Do not throttle to real time.
.TP
.BI \-\-frame\-us= N
Wall-clock microseconds per frame.
Default 16666; 0 is
.BR \-\-fast .
.TP
.BR \-\-turbo [ =report ]
.B \-\-fast
plus fast-forwarding of spin loops that cannot change anything, and dropping
a full GPU pixel queue instead of painting it (a screenshot may then be
incomplete).
.B =report
prints what was skipped to standard error at exit.
Off while anything watches single instructions (breakpoints, watchpoints,
stepping, profiling, tracing).
.TP
.B \-\-gpu\-eager
Paint every GPU command at once instead of when a screenshot or save needs
the pixels.
The pictures are the same.
.TP
.BR \-\-fixed\-clock [ =\fIY,D,S\fP ]
.B TIM_CurrentDate
and
.B TIM_CurrentTime
start at year
.IR Y ,
day
.I D
(0 is January 1st) and
.I S
seconds, default 2026,0,0, instead of the host clock.
.TP
.BI \-\-seed= N
Seed the
.B RNG_CurrentValue
generator with
.IR N .
.TP
.B \-\-fixed\-cycles
Reads of
.B TIM_CycleCounter
return 0.
.TP
.B \-\-warn\-halt
Say on standard error when the program executes HLT (the emulator then stops).
.TP
.BI \-\-halt\-status= N
Exit with status
.I N
(1 to 255) when the run ends at HLT and nothing else went wrong.
.SS Headless, batch and unattended
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-batch ", " \-\-quiet
Script mode: commands come from standard input or
.B \-C
without prompt or echo; no banner, instruction line, display list or final
register summary.
Standard output holds only what
.BR print ,
.BR console ", ..."
print; acknowledgements, stop reasons and diagnostics go to standard error.
Blank lines are skipped and the end of input ends the run.
A failed
.B expect
or an
.B until
that times out ends the run with status 1; a command that cannot be parsed
ends it with status 4.
.TP
.BI \-\-expect\-reach= LABEL [, LABEL ...]
The run must reach one of these code labels of the debug files.
When it does, print
.B EXPECT REACHED
.I label
.BI frame= N
.BI ip=0x ........
on standard output, stop and exit 0.
If the run ends any other way (frame limit, HLT, a CPU error,
.BR quit )
print
.B EXPECT MISSED
and the labels, and exit 1 (also with
.BR \-x ).
An unknown label is a startup error (exit status 1).
.TP
.BR \-x ", " \-\-exit\-status
The exit status tells how the program ended (see
.BR "EXIT STATUS" ).
.TP
.BI \-\-dump= N [@ ADDR ]
.TP
.IB ADDR : N
After the run print
.I N
RAM words (signed, one per line) from
.IR ADDR :
hexadecimal, or a label (also tried as
.BR global_ NAME );
default the start of RAM.
.TP
.B \-\-console
Print the BIOS-font text screen when the run ends.
.SS Input
.TP
.BI \-\-input= SCRIPT
Hold gamepad buttons by cartridge frame (0 is the first frame the cartridge
runs):
.IB BUTTON [+ BUTTON ]@ FIRST \- LAST ,...
(or
.BI @ FRAME\fR).
Buttons are LEFT RIGHT UP DOWN START A B X Y L R (SELECT is X).
.BI @ FILE
takes the
.B V32_INPUT=
line of a V32TRACE report.
Default:
.BR $V32_INPUT .
.TP
.BI \-\-input\-port= N
Gamepad port that
.B \-\-input
drives, default 0.
.TP
.BI \-\-input2= SCRIPT
The same for gamepad 2 (port 1); default
.BR $V32_INPUT2 .
.TP
.BR \-\-v32kbd [ =\fIPORT\fP ]
Emulate a v32io keyboard on gamepad
.I PORT
(0 to 3, default 0).
.TP
.BI \-\-kbd\-input= SOURCE
Host key source:
.BR stdin ,
.BI fd: N
or a FIFO, pipe or file path.
Implies
.BR \-\-v32kbd .
.TP
.B \-\-kbd\-sync
Deliver the next key event only after the program has read the gamepad since
the last one (by default a new event replaces an unread one, as the real
keyboard does).
.TP
.BR \-\-v32mouse [ =\fIPORT\fP ]
Emulate a v32io mouse on gamepad
.I PORT
(0 to 3, default 1); drive it with the
.B mouse
command.
.TP
.B \-\-tty
Full-screen interactive mode: the text console on this terminal and the keys
to a v32kbd (port 0 unless
.BR \-\-v32kbd=\fIN\fP ).
Implies
.BR \-r .
Ctrl-] returns to the debugger prompt;
.B continue
goes back.
.SS Memory card and v32dev
.TP
.BR \-\-v32dev\-doorbell [ =\fIMS\fP ]
v32dev mailbox doorbell: header flag bit 1, per-channel reply counters at
header words 10 to 13, and a store to a cart part's
.B c2h_head
rings: flush, give the host
.I MS
milliseconds (default 2; 0 is no wait) to answer, import its reply, go on.
.TP
.BI \-\-v32dev\-host= N = SPEC
Serve channel
.I N
(0 to 3) of the card's v32dev mailbox natively; may be repeated.
Needs a card given with
.BR \-M .
.I SPEC
is
.BI storage: FILE
with optional
.BR ,bytes ,
.BR ,ro ,
.BI ,sector= N
and
.BI ,create= S \fR;
or
.BR serial:stdio ", " serial:echo
or
.BI serial:tcp= PORT
(127.0.0.1 only, one client at a time) with optional
.BI ,hup= N ,
.BI ,in= FILE ,
.BI ,out= FILE
and
.BR ,hangup .
.TP
.B \-\-v32dev\-host\-force
Format the card over other data in its last 64 KiB.
.SS Output, screenshots and the text console
.TP
.BI \-\-screenshot\-at= FRAMES
Write the screen as PNG after these cartridge frames:
.IR N ,
.I A\-B
or
.IR A\-B/STEP ,
comma separated.
Frame 0 is the first frame that ends with the program counter in the
cartridge, that is after the BIOS has handed over (StandardBios's logo takes
about 250 frames, which
.B \-\-max\-frames
also counts; the bundled StubBios hands over at frame 2).
Frames the run never reached are reported on standard error at exit.
.TP
.BI \-\-screenshot\-file= PATTERN
File name for those;
.B %d
is the frame.
Default
.IR v32sim\-shot\-%05d.png .
.TP
.BR \-\-screengrid [ =\fISPEC\fP ]
Grid over screenshots:
.IR DX [x DY ],
.BI color= C ,
.BI alpha= N ,
.BR labels | nolabels ,
comma separated.
Default 40x40.
.TP
.BI \-\-highlight= T : R [: COLOR ]
Outline texture
.I T
region
.I R
(or
.BI bios: R\fR)
in screenshots; comma separated.
.TP
.B \-\-noextra
No grid and no outlines in screenshots.
.TP
.BI \-\-console\-color= MODE
Text console colors:
.BR none ,
.BR 16 ,
.BR 256 ,
.B true
or
.B auto
(the default with
.BR \-\-tty ).
.TP
.BI \-\-cursor\-color= COLOR
Recolor the cursor block (BIOS character 20): a color name,
.I #RRGGBB
or
.BR off .
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
More verbose output, including
.RB " [v32sim] BIOS: " \fIPATH\fP.
.TP
.BR \-D ", " \-\-debug
Simulator debug output (development).
.SS Profiling, statistics and traces
.TP
.BI \-\-frame\-stats= FILE
.TP
.BI \-\-stats= FILE
One CSV row per frame (cycles, CPU %, GPU pixels, GPU %, then the GPU, SPU
and bus counters), and a summary (mean, p50, p95, p99, maximum, total, the
busiest regions) on standard error at exit.
.TP
.BI \-\-frame\-stats\-skip= N
.TP
.BI \-\-stats\-skip= N
Leave the first
.I N
frames (StandardBios's logo takes about 250) out of the summary.
.TP
.BI \-\-frame\-stats\-top= N
.TP
.BI \-\-stats\-top= N
List the
.I N
regions charged the most GPU pixels; default 8, 0 is none.
.TP
.BI \-\-stats\-format= FMT
.B csv
(default) or
.BR json :
JSON Lines with stable dotted names and a summary line last.
.TP
.BI \-\-heap[= PROG.asm ]
For
.B v32lua
cartridges: at the end of every frame print on standard output
.BI "HEAP F" N " ptr=0x" ... " used=" W " peak=" W "[ free=" W " blocks=" N ]
(heap pointer, words used since
.BR HEAP_START ,
largest use so far; the peak is checked after every instruction), with
.B \-\-gc arena
also
.BR floor= ", " pin= ", " depth= " and " stack= .
At exit:
.B HEAP PEAK
and
.BR "HEAP END" ,
the same format as
.BR "v32orun \-\-heap" ,
whose frame numbers differ (these are the console's, BIOS frames included).
.B used
is the heap top and counts the blocks on
.BR v32lua 's
free lists;
.BR free= " and " blocks=
(when the
.B .asm
has
.BR FREE_MASK " and " FREE_BINS )
show how much of it is reusable, so
.B used \- free
is live memory.
The
.B %define
symbols are read from the cartridge's
.I NAME.asm
(searched like the debug files) or from
.IR PROG.asm .
Only reads RAM.
.TP
.BI \-\-profile\-out= FILE
At the end of the run write how many times each instruction address was
executed.
Needs no prompt and no
.BR \-p .
Debug labels add a third column.
.TP
.BI \-\-profile\-format= FMT
.B text
(default) or
.BR bin :
a
.B V32PROF1
header, then little-endian 32-bit (address, count) pairs.
.TP
.BI \-\-profile\-top= N
Print the
.I N
hottest addresses (or functions) to standard error at the end.
.TP
.BI \-\-profile\-by= KIND
.B address
(default) or
.BR function :
add up the addresses of each function (the closest label).
.TP
.BI \-\-profile\-event= KIND
What is counted:
.B instr
(default),
.BR gpu\-draw ,
.BR gpu\-portwrite ,
.B spu\-write
or
.BR cycles .
.TP
.B \-\-time\-breakdown
At exit, where v32sim's own wall-clock time goes per frame (cpu, gpu, spu,
output).
.TP
.BI \-\-frame\-log= FILE
With it, a row per frame.
.TP
.BI \-\-gpu\-trace= FILE
.TP
.BI \-\-spu\-trace= FILE
Record the program's GPU (SPU) port writes by frame, in a binary format
(docs/TRACE\-FORMAT.md).
.TP
.BI \-\-trace\-dump= FILE
Print a trace file as text and exit.
.TP
.BI \-\-v32trace= PREFIX
V32TRACE recorder: read the translator's
.I <cart>.v32trace.meta
and labels, watch its trap routines and events, write
.IR PREFIX .traps.txt,
.IR PREFIX .blocks.txt
and the report
.IR PREFIX .trace.txt.
After a trap the cartridge restarts.
A meta line
.B inputclock ADDR
(spec 2.3) adds the buttons keyed by the guest's frame clock (V32_INPUT_CLOCK) and clock= fields; the report's [perf] and marker stretches carry a load: line.
.TP
.BI \-\-v32trace\-meta= FILE
.TP
.BI \-\-v32trace\-labels= FILE
Name those files when they are not beside the cartridge.
.TP
.B \-\-v32trace\-norestart
Stay in the trap.
.SH DEBUGGER COMMANDS
At the prompt, and in files given with
.B \-C
or on standard input in batch mode.
Commands may be abbreviated (s is step, n next, c continue, p print,
se set, pro profile, in inventory, lo load); a blank line repeats the
previous step, next or continue; a line starting with # is a comment.
The single letters are b break, c continue, d display, g gamepad, h help,
i ignore, l label, n next, p print, q quit, r replace, s step, w watch, x x and
? help.
Where several commands start with the same letters the first one in the
command table wins, so r is replace, not run (ru is run).
Formats (\fB/b /B /d /D /f /o /u /x /X\fR) follow
.BR print ,
.BR x ,
.B display
and
.BR watch .
.B help
.RI [ COMMAND ]
or
.B ?
describes each in full.
.SS Running and stopping
.TP
.B step
Execute one instruction; in the source modes, one source line.
.TP
.B next
As step, but a call counts as one step.
.TP
.B continue
Resume until a breakpoint, watchpoint or the end.
.TP
.BR run " \fIN\fP, " wait " \fIN\fP"
Resume for
.I N
frames, then stop.
.TP
.BR until " [" \fIFILE:LINE ]
Run to a later line (leave a loop), or to
.IR FILE:LINE .
.TP
.BR until " \(dq\fITEXT\fP\(dq [" timeout " \fIFRAMES\fP] [" rows " \fIN\fP]"
Resume until the text console shows
.IR TEXT .
.TP
.B finish
Run until the current function returns.
.TP
.B ignore
Skip the current instruction.
.TP
.BR frame ", " up ", " down " [\fIN\fP]"
Select a stack frame.
.TP
.BR backtrace ", " bt
List of calls, with source lines when known.
.TP
.BR quit " [\fISTATUS\fP]"
Leave the simulator with exit status
.IR STATUS .
.SS Breakpoints, watchpoints, checks
.TP
.BR break " [\fI0xADDR\fP|\fILABEL\fP|\fIFILE:LINE\fP]"
Set a breakpoint, or list them.
.TP
.BR tbreak " [\fIFILE:LINE\fP|\fILINE\fP|\fI0xADDR\fP|\fILABEL\fP]"
One-shot breakpoint; with no argument it lists the breakpoints, as
.B break
does.
.TP
.BR unbreak " \fI#\fP"
Remove breakpoint
.IR # .
.TP
.BR watch [ /d "] \fILOCATION OP VALUE\fP [\fILABEL\fP]"
Break when the comparison becomes true; location is a register, IOPort,
address or symbol.
.TP
.B watchlist
List watchpoints.
.TP
.BR unwatch " \fIINDEX\fP|\fILABEL\fP"
Remove a watchpoint.
.TP
.BR expect [ /d "] \fILOCATION OP VALUE\fP"
.TP
.BR expect " console \(dq\fITEXT\fP\(dq [" rows " \fIN\fP]"
.TP
.BR expect " stats \fINAME\fP[.\fIAGG\fP] \fIOP VALUE\fP"
Check now; a failure sets exit status 1 and ends a batch run.
.SS Inspecting
.TP
.BR print [ /\fIF\fP "] \fIXYZ\fP"
One-time display of a register, address, IOPort, label or C global.
.TP
.BR x [ /\fIF\fP "] \fISYMBOL\fP|\fI0xADDR\fP [\fICOUNT\fP]"
Display
.I COUNT
words.
.TP
.BR display [ /\fIF\fP "] \fIXYZ\fP [\fILABEL\fP]"
Add a display list item.
.TP
.BR undisplay " \fI#\fP"
Remove it.
.TP
.BR locals " [\fINAME\fP], " info " locals [\fINAME\fP]"
Local variables of the selected frame.
.TP
.BR list " [\fIFILE:LINE\fP|\fIFUNC\fP]"
Source around the current line.
.TP
.B inventory
Loaded components and resources.
.TP
.B profile
Profiling report.
.TP
.B version
The simulator version.
.TP
.BR label " [\fI0xADDR LABEL\fP], " unlabel " \fI#\fP"
List, add and remove labels.
.TP
.BR stats " [" show | regions | reset | on | off | save "\fI...\fP]"
GPU, SPU and bus counters per frame and per region.
.SS Changing state
.TP
.BR set " [\fINAME\fP=\fIVALUE\fP]"
Set a setting (color, deref, profile, debug, errorchk, verbose, source,
mode), a register,
.IR 0xADDR ,
or an IOPort; with no argument, list the settings.
.TP
.BR asm " [@\fIADDR\fP|\-x] \fIINSTRUCTION\fP"
Assemble an instruction: patch memory, or run it once with
.BR \-x .
.TP
.BR replace " IP:\fIX\fP IR:\fIY\fP IV:\fIZ\fP"
Patch instruction memory.
.TP
.BR save " [\fIFILE\fP], " restore " \fIFILE\fP"
Write and restore the machine state.
.TP
.BR load " bios|cart|memc:\fIFILE\fP, " unload " bios|cart|memc"
Swap components.
.TP
.BR gamepad " [\fI#\fP] [\fICOMMAND\fP]"
View or simulate gamepad state.
.TP
.BR kbd " [\fISUBCOMMAND\fP]"
v32kbd keyboard:
.BR attach ,
.BR detach ,
.BR type ,
.BR key ,
.BR input ,
.BR clear ,
.BR sync .
.TP
.BR mouse " [\fISUBCOMMAND\fP]"
v32mouse:
.BR attach ,
.BR detach ,
.BR move ,
.BR down ,
.BR up ,
.BR click ,
.BR wait ,
.BR clear .
.TP
.BR mailbox " [\fICHANNEL\fP]"
v32dev mailbox state of the memory card.
.TP
.BR dev " [\fISUBCOMMAND\fP]"
Native v32dev host:
.BR info ,
.BR format ,
.BR attach ,
.BR detach ,
.BR swap .
.SS Screen
.TP
.BR console " [\fIFILE\fP]"
Show the BIOS-font text drawn on the screen as text (or write it to
.IR FILE ).
Also
.BR "console \-\-attrs" [ =fg | bg | all ],
.B console color
.IR MODE ,
.B console cursor
.IR COLOR ,
.BR "console status" .
.TP
.BR screenshot " [\-\-plain] [\fIFILE\fP]"
Write the GPU screen to a PNG file.
.TP
.BR highlight " [\fITEX:REGION\fP [\fICOLOR\fP]]"
Outline regions in screenshots (also called
.BR boundbox ).
.TP
.BR screengrid " [\fIARGS\fP]"
Lay a grid over screenshots.
.TP
.BR noextra " [on|off]"
Screenshots without grid and outlines.
.TP
.BR mark " [\fINOTE\fP]"
With
.BR \-\-v32trace :
drop a marker in the report.
.TP
.BR help " [\fICOMMAND\fP], " ?
Display help.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B V32BIOS
The BIOS to load when
.B \-B
is not given, tried before the StandardBios install and the bundled StubBios.
Also read by the scripts in
.I tools/
and
.IR tests/ .
.TP
.BR V32_INPUT ", " V32_INPUT2
Gamepad scripts used when
.B \-\-input
or
.B \-\-input2
is not given.
.TP
.B V32DEV_DISABLE
Set to anything but
.BR 0 :
the memory card is an ordinary card with no v32dev mailbox.
.TP
.B V32DEV_SERVER
Set to anything but
.BR 0 :
the built-in v32dev server of the DesktopEmulator patch of the v32io project.
The card needs no formatting, the mailbox lives in memory only and never reaches
the card file, and the channels come from
.BR V32DEV_CH0 " to " V32DEV_CH3 .
Unset or
.BR 0 :
those variables are not looked at.
.TP
.BR V32DEV_CH0 " to " V32DEV_CH3
With
.BR V32DEV_SERVER :
what channel N serves, a SPEC as for
.BR \-\-v32dev\-host ,
which wins for its channel.
.TP
.BR NO_COLOR ", " COLORTERM ", " TERM
Choose the level of
.BR \-\-console\-color=auto :
none if
.B NO_COLOR
is set and not empty; true color for
.B COLORTERM
containing truecolor or 24bit; 256 colors for a
.B TERM
containing 256color; 16 for any other terminal except dumb.
.TP
.BR HOME ", " INPUTRC
Read by GNU readline for the prompt.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/local/Vircon32/Emulator/Bios/StandardBios.v32
The default BIOS when
.B $V32BIOS
is not set.
.TP
.I StubBios.v32
The bundled BIOS, used when neither of the above exists, with its
.IR StubBios.vbin.debug .
Searched for beside the binary (release zip),
in
.I ../bios/
of a checkout's
.IR bin/ ,
in
.I ~/.local/lib/v32sim/bios/
.RB ( make\ install )
and in
.I $(VROOT)/v32sim/bios/
.RB ( make\ sysinstall ).
See
.IR bios/README.md .
.TP
.IR NAME .vbin.debug ", " NAME .asm.debug
Labels and line information, searched for as described under
.BR \-\-debug\-dir .
.TP
.IR NAME .asm.sym ", " NAME .vbin.sym
Data symbols.
.TP
.IR NAME .vbin.vars
Local variables, for
.BR locals .
.TP
.IR NAME .v32trace.meta
V32TRACE translator metadata.
.TP
.IR v32sim\-shot\-NNNNN.png
Screenshots of
.BR \-\-screenshot\-at .
.TP
.IR v32sim.YYYYMMDD\-HHMMSS .png ", " .sav
Default names of the
.B screenshot
and
.B save
commands.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The program executed HLT (unless
.BR \-\-halt\-status ),
or
.B quit
or the end of input.
.TP
.B 1
An
.B expect
failed, an
.B until
timed out, or
.B \-\-expect\-reach
did not reach its label (it outranks 2 and 3).
At startup also: a bad option or option value (an unknown
.B \-L
mode too), a
.B \-C
file that cannot be opened,
.B \-\-tty
without a terminal, a bad
.BR \-\-v32dev\-host .
.TP
.B 2
The frame limit was reached
.RB ( \-x ).
Also the status when /dev/null cannot be opened at startup.
.TP
.B 3
A system error
.RB ( \-x ):
the run stops instead of entering the BIOS error handler.
.TP
.B 4
.B \-\-batch
could not parse a command.
.TP
.B 5
.B \-\-dump
could not print what was asked.
.TP
.B 11
Startup: a BIOS, cartridge or memory card file cannot be opened.
.TP
.B 12
Startup: such a file cannot be read.
.TP
.B 21
Startup: such a file is not a valid V32 file.
.TP
.BR 4 ", " 5 ", " 7 ", " 13 ", " 14 ", " 15
Internal failures (out of memory, an invalid IOPort access mode) with an
[ERROR] message on standard error; they should not happen.
4 and 5 are also the statuses above.
.TP
.I N
.B quit
.I N
overrides the above;
.BI \-\-halt\-status= N
sets the status of an HLT.
.SH EXAMPLES
Debug a cartridge, stopping at a label:
.PP
.RS
.EX
v32sim \-B StandardBios.v32 \-b main cart.v32
.EE
.RE
.PP
Run headless for 600 frames, print four words of a C global, and use the
exit status:
.PP
.RS
.EX
v32sim \-B StandardBios.v32 \-r \-q \-x \-\-fast \-\-max\-frames=600 \e
    \-\-dump=4@counter cart.v32 </dev/null; echo $?
.EE
.RE
.PP
Screenshots after cartridge frames 60 and 120 with a button held:
.PP
.RS
.EX
v32sim \-B StandardBios.v32 \-r \-q \-\-fast \-\-max\-frames=400 \e
    \-\-input=A@40\-44 \-\-screenshot\-at=60,120 cart.v32 </dev/null
.EE
.RE
.PP
Frame statistics and the hottest functions:
.PP
.RS
.EX
v32sim \-B StandardBios.v32 \-r \-q \-\-fast \-\-max\-frames=1800 \e
    \-\-frame\-stats=load.csv \-\-frame\-stats\-skip=250 \e
    \-\-profile\-by=function \-\-profile\-top=20 cart.v32 </dev/null
.EE
.RE
.PP
A scripted session on a program with a prompt:
.PP
.RS
.EX
v32sim \-B StandardBios.v32 \-\-batch \-\-fast \-x \-\-v32kbd dos.v32 < session.cmd
.EE
.RE
.PP
A text program on the terminal:
.PP
.RS
.EX
v32sim \-\-tty \-B StandardBios.v32 v32nix.v32
.EE
.RE
